## Overview

This chart deploys the [Pacto Operator](https://github.com/TrianaLab/pacto/tree/main/integrations/kubernetes). It tells you, for each service in your cluster, whether what is running still matches its [Pacto](https://github.com/TrianaLab/pacto) contract. With the optional node agent (`nodeAgent.enabled=true`), it also checks what each service actually does on the network against its contract, and reports only what it can prove. See [Runtime conformance](https://pacto.run/latest/integrations/kubernetes/runtime-conformance/).

What the chart installs:

- Controller `Deployment` with health and readiness probes
- RBAC: a `ServiceAccount`, a cluster-scoped `ClusterRole` + `ClusterRoleBinding` (reads, and the dashboard's ClusterRole), a manager `Role` + `RoleBinding` in the release namespace (the dashboard and Evidence Server lifecycle) and a namespaced leader-election `Role` + `RoleBinding`
- Metrics `Service` on port 8443 (HTTPS by default with the chart's `metrics.secure=true`; see [Scraping the metrics](https://pacto.run/latest/integrations/kubernetes/installation/#scraping-the-metrics))
- CRDs: `Pacto`, `PactoRevision` and `NodeRuntimeObservation`, which `helm upgrade` updates and `helm uninstall` keeps (`crds.install=false` leaves them to you)
- Optional: Prometheus `ServiceMonitor`
- Optional: the node agent `DaemonSet` (`nodeAgent.enabled`, off by default), in its own namespace (`nodeAgent.namespace`, default `pacto-node-agent`) that the chart creates privileged for Pod Security, with a `ValidatingAdmissionPolicy` binding each agent's writes to its own node
- Optional: upstream Flux 2.10.1 (`idp.flux.install`, profile idp only), its source, kustomize and helm controllers vendored under `files/flux/` and upgraded with this release. Privilege: the Flux ClusterRoles, the cluster-admin ClusterRoleBinding upstream gives kustomize-controller and helm-controller (each applier object runs as its namespace's ServiceAccount under the lockdown) and the CRDs. Uninstalling Pacto or turning the value off leaves Flux running; see [IDP mode](https://pacto.run/latest/integrations/kubernetes/delivery-setup/#idp-mode) for the exit path

The operator controller never modifies your workloads. It only reads them and reports whether they match their contracts. The same chart ships an opt-in delivery component (alpha, `delivery.enabled`), its own `Deployment`, which decides which version runs where and moves Flux or Argo CD source pointers. Its own admission policies guard each promotion. See [Delivery](https://pacto.run/latest/integrations/kubernetes/delivery/).

## Installation

```bash
helm install pacto-operator oci://ghcr.io/trianalab/pacto/charts/pacto-operator \
  --version {{ template "chart.version" . }} \
  --namespace pacto-operator-system --create-namespace
```

[Install the operator](https://pacto.run/latest/integrations/kubernetes/installation/) covers pinning a version, common overrides and checking the install. [Upgrade the operator](https://pacto.run/latest/integrations/kubernetes/upgrade/#hand-older-crds-to-helm) covers the one-time step for CRDs an older chart installed from its `crds/` directory.

## Dashboard

The dashboard is **enabled by default**. The operator manages the dashboard Deployment, internal Service (`pacto-dashboard`, ClusterIP), ServiceAccount, and RBAC.

The dashboard image is **injected into the controller binary at build time via ldflags** (a `dashboardImage` variable in the `cmd` package), which couples the dashboard version to the Pacto library the controller was built against. It is **not** overridable through Helm values or any runtime flag. If the dashboard is enabled but the binary was built without this ldflag (so `dashboardImage` is empty), the controller fails to start — the dashboard cannot be deployed from a binary that lacks an embedded image reference.

The chart creates a separate exposure Service (`pacto-operator-dashboard` for the documented `pacto-operator` release; the name is `<fullname>-dashboard`) with configurable type, plus optional Ingress and HTTPRoute resources. The operator owns the lifecycle; the chart owns the networking. These are distinct concerns:

| Resource | Managed by | Purpose |
|----------|-----------|---------|
| `pacto-dashboard` Service | Operator | Internal ClusterIP on port 3000, always present when dashboard is enabled |
| `pacto-operator-dashboard` Service (`<fullname>-dashboard`) | Chart | Configurable type (ClusterIP/NodePort/LoadBalancer) on port 3000 (`dashboard.service.port`), backend for Ingress/HTTPRoute |
| Ingress | Chart | Optional, references the chart-managed Service |
| HTTPRoute | Chart | Optional, references the chart-managed Service. When `rules` is empty, a catch-all rule routes all traffic to the dashboard |

In total a chart install can stand up **three** Services: the metrics Service on port 8443 (see [Scraping the metrics](https://pacto.run/latest/integrations/kubernetes/installation/#scraping-the-metrics)), the operator-managed internal `pacto-dashboard` ClusterIP on port 3000, and the chart-managed dashboard exposure Service on port 3000 (whose type is configurable via `dashboard.service.type`).

### Private OCI Registries

There are two independent credential paths for private registries:

| Credential | Used by | Configured via |
|------------|---------|----------------|
| `spec.contractRef.pullSecretRef` | **Operator** (pulling contracts during reconciliation) | Per-Pacto CR field |
| `dashboard.ociSecrets` | **Dashboard** pod (browsing contracts in the UI) | Helm value |

All three secret formats are supported in both paths:

- **Opaque** with `registry` + `token` keys — bearer/registry token (e.g. a GitHub PAT)
- **Opaque** with `registry` + `username` + `password` keys — basic auth
- **`kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson`** — standard Docker registry auth (supports multiple registries per secret)

> **Note:** For dashboard credentials, Opaque secrets **must** include a `registry` key specifying the target hostname (e.g. `ghcr.io`). This is required because go-containerregistry performs exact hostname matching. For operator credentials (per-Pacto CR), the `registry` key is not needed — the operator infers it from the OCI reference.

#### Operator credentials (per Pacto CR)

Create the Secret in the same namespace as the Pacto CR and name it in `spec.contractRef.pullSecretRef`. The supported Secret types and the `registry` key are in [Contract bindings](https://pacto.run/latest/integrations/kubernetes/contract-bindings/).

The operator reads a pull Secret, or a Secret that backs a contract configuration, only where the chart grants it: in the release namespace, in each of `controller.watchNamespaces`, and the Secrets `controller.secretGrants` names as `namespace/name` (each through a Role limited to that Secret). No ClusterRole grants Secrets. A pull Secret outside those grants sets the Pacto's `ContractValid` condition to `Unknown` with reason `PullSecretNotGranted`, and a configuration Secret the operator may not read reads Unknown with a finding that names the missing grant.

```yaml
controller:
  secretGrants:
    - payments/regcred
```

#### Dashboard credentials (Helm value)

To give the dashboard pod access to private registries, create Secrets in the **operator namespace** and configure them via Helm values. The operator reads these source secrets, merges their credentials, and creates a managed `dockerconfigjson` secret that the dashboard pod mounts automatically.

```yaml
dashboard:
  ociSecrets:
    - ghcr-creds
    - ecr-creds
```

Credentials from all secrets are merged; later secrets override earlier ones for the same registry.

```bash
# Option 1: dockerconfigjson (recommended for multi-registry)
kubectl create secret docker-registry ghcr-creds \
  --docker-server=ghcr.io \
  --docker-username=x-access-token \
  --docker-password="$(gh auth token)" \
  -n pacto-operator-system

# Option 2: Opaque with registry key
kubectl create secret generic ghcr-creds \
  --from-literal=registry=ghcr.io \
  --from-literal=username=x-access-token \
  --from-literal=password="$(gh auth token)" \
  -n pacto-operator-system
```

To disable the dashboard:

```yaml
dashboard:
  enabled: false
```

### Accessing the dashboard

**Port-forward** (default, no extra config):

```bash
kubectl port-forward svc/pacto-operator-dashboard 3000:3000 -n pacto-operator-system
```

**NodePort**:

```yaml
dashboard:
  service:
    type: NodePort
    nodePort: 30300
```

**LoadBalancer**:

```yaml
dashboard:
  service:
    type: LoadBalancer
```

**Ingress**:

```yaml
dashboard:
  ingress:
    enabled: true
    className: nginx
    hosts:
      - host: pacto.example.com
        paths:
          - path: /
            pathType: Prefix
    tls:
      - secretName: pacto-tls
        hosts:
          - pacto.example.com
```

**HTTPRoute (Gateway API)**:

```yaml
dashboard:
  httpRoute:
    enabled: true
    parentRefs:
      - name: main-gateway
        namespace: gateway-system
    hostnames:
      - pacto.example.com
```

The dashboard is always deployed into the release namespace. Its observation scope is inherited from `controller.watchNamespaces` — there is no separate dashboard scope configuration.

### Observation Scope

The dashboard inherits the controller's scope, `controller.watchNamespaces`; with a list it reads only those namespaces and reviews people with namespaced LocalSubjectAccessReviews. See [Common overrides](https://pacto.run/latest/integrations/kubernetes/installation/#common-overrides).

## Security

The chart follows security best practices by default:

- Runs as non-root (UID 65532, distroless base image)
- Read-only root filesystem
- All capabilities dropped
- Seccomp profile set to `RuntimeDefault`
- Metrics endpoint served over HTTPS with authentication (chart default `metrics.secure=true`); see [Scraping the metrics](https://pacto.run/latest/integrations/kubernetes/installation/#scraping-the-metrics)

### Profile idp

`profile: idp` turns each piece on only where its prerequisite holds; `pacto fleet providers` says why each one is on or off. See [What idp turns on](https://pacto.run/latest/integrations/kubernetes/profiles/#what-idp-turns-on-when-the-cluster-supports-it).

| Piece | Prerequisite | Turn it off |
|---|---|---|
| Evidence Server | The registry serves OCI Referrers, a subject or subject prefix is set on that registry (default `oci://<registry>/` when `registry` has a path) and the trust Secret (`<fullname>-evidence-trust` or `evidence.trust.existingSecret`) holds a non-empty `.pub` key, or the `evidence.trust.configKey` key when that is set | Remove the keys from the trust Secret, or leave profile idp |
| Recorder | A streams repository and exactly one complete signing method, with `delivery.recorder.enabled` unset | `delivery.recorder.enabled=false` |
| Upstream Crossplane | `idp.crossplane.install=true`; refused outside idp, under hardened and where another Crossplane runs | `idp.crossplane.install=false`, which keeps Crossplane's CRDs and resources |

The privilege idp adds:

- Pacto delivery's ClusterRoles, and the recorder's ClusterRole when the recorder runs
- The read of ServiceMonitors and PodMonitors
- `create`, `delete`, `get`, `patch` and `update` on Deployments and Services in the release namespace, for the Evidence Server
- With `idp.crossplane.install=true` only, the upstream Crossplane chart's RBAC: the `crossplane` ServiceAccount writes CRDs, Secrets, ServiceAccounts, Services, Deployments and webhook configurations cluster-wide, the `rbac-manager` ServiceAccount may escalate and bind ClusterRoles, and the `crossplane:masters` group is bound to `crossplane-admin`

## Artifact Verification

The chart and the controller image are signed keyless with Sigstore. [Published artifacts](https://pacto.run/latest/integrations/kubernetes/artifact-hub/) gives the coordinates and the verify command.

